1. Establish CLI readiness and authentication.
   - Confirm required tools exist:
     - `git --version`
     - `gh --version`
   - Confirm GitHub CLI authentication is valid:
     - `gh auth status`
   - If authentication is invalid, stop before push/release operations and
     require the user to re-authenticate with:
     - `gh auth login`

2. Enter the target repository and verify branch context.
   - Move into the repo root:
     - `cd /path/to/repo`
   - Confirm current branch:
     - `git branch --show-current`
   - Confirm remote:
     - `git remote -v`

3. Review worktree state before release work begins.
   - Check for tracked or staged changes:
     - `git status --short`
   - If unrelated tracked changes exist, stop unless the user explicitly wants a
     dirty-tree release.
   - Ignore unrelated untracked files by default.
   - If the only intended local additions are release-note or prompt files under
     `prompts/`, that is acceptable when the user explicitly wants them included.

4. Sync local state with the release target branch.
   - Fetch all refs and tags:
     - `git fetch --all --tags --prune`
   - Switch to the intended release branch:
     - `git checkout main`
   - Pull latest changes:
     - `git pull origin main`

5. Discover repository context before writing release notes.
   - Read high-signal files if present:
     - `README.md`
     - `.github/workflows/www.pages.yml`
     - prior release payloads under `prompts/release.*.md`
     - `prompts/release.style.prompt`
   - Inspect recent tags:
     - `git tag --sort=-creatordate | head -n 10`
   - Resolve the most recent tag if one exists:
     - `PREV_TAG=$(git describe --tags --abbrev=0 2>/dev/null || true)`

6. Collect change context.
   - Prefer the previous tag-to-HEAD range:
     - `git log "${PREV_TAG}..HEAD" --decorate --stat --oneline`
     - `git log "${PREV_TAG}..HEAD" --name-only --pretty=format:'COMMIT:%H%nSUBJECT:%s%nBODY:%b%n'`
     - `git diff --stat "${PREV_TAG}..HEAD"`
   - If no previous tag exists, fall back to the last 10 commits:
     - `git log -10 --decorate --stat --oneline`
     - `git log -10 --name-only --pretty=format:'COMMIT:%H%nSUBJECT:%s%nBODY:%b%n'`

7. Infer the next version conservatively.
   - This repo currently uses bare tags such as `0.0.1` and `0.0.2`.
   - Do not prepend `v` unless the repo’s tag convention changes.
   - Default policy:
     - `feat` or shipped capability expansion in the current `0.0.x` line may
       still remain a patch bump when the project is intentionally iterating
       through early `0.0.x` releases.
     - `fix` => patch bump
     - `BREAKING CHANGE` or explicit major policy shift => stop and confirm
   - If the version choice is ambiguous, propose the next version and reason
     before tagging.

8. Generate the release notes markdown document.
   - Follow `prompts/release.style.prompt`.
   - Write the long-form GitHub release body to:
     - `prompts/release.<version>.md`
   - Include:
     - summary paragraphs
     - scope
     - highlights
     - added / changed / fixed
     - chosen `#COMMIT`
     - commit-range summary
     - assets

9. Decide the commit message for release metadata updates.
   - Follow `prompts/commit.command.prompt` and `prompts/commit.style.prompt`.
   - If the changes are mostly release notes and release-prompt maintenance,
     prefer a release-scoped message such as:
     - `release:0.0.3 - add vlan release notes and prompt conventions`

10. Stage release-note and prompt files explicitly.
   - Because `prompts/` is gitignored in this repo, use force-add only for the
     specific files the user wants committed:
     - `git add -f prompts/<file>`

11. Commit only after the release notes and version are finalized.
   - Example:
     - `git commit -m "release:X.Y.Z - ..."`

12. Create the tag locally only after the commit exists.
   - Annotated tag:
     - `git tag -a "X.Y.Z" -m "X.Y.Z"`

13. Push commit and tag only after authentication is valid.
   - Push branch:
     - `git push origin main`
   - Push tag:
     - `git push origin "X.Y.Z"`

14. Create the GitHub release as a draft first unless explicitly told to publish immediately.
   - Use the markdown document as the release body:
     - `gh release create "X.Y.Z" --target main --title "X.Y.Z" --notes-file "prompts/release.X.Y.Z.md" --draft`

15. Perform a final verification pass before publish.
   - Confirm:
     - tag points at correct commit:
       - `git rev-list -n 1 "X.Y.Z"`
     - release exists:
       - `gh release view "X.Y.Z"`
     - notes are accurate and not duplicated

16. Publish only after final confirmation.
   - If the draft is approved:
     - `gh release edit "X.Y.Z" --draft=false`

17. Output policy for the LLM.
   - Return:
     - proposed version
     - proposed commit message
     - path to the generated markdown notes
     - exact git/tag/release commands
     - any auth or push blockers
   - If auth is invalid, complete local note creation and local git work first,
     then stop before the remote release operations.
